# nodus/oevm/tests/target/cad-rearm.env — rete#240.2: an adopted cadence is a
# KEPT cadence, over the real pulse mailbox.
#
# The probe starts the kernel base tick at 2000 ms and, from INSIDE tick 3's own
# reaction (the real shape — applyLivenessGoal runs inside the tick's reaction
# chain on a bridged node), adopts 5000 ms through the same adoptCad:bud: door
# goal/liveness uses. It then certifies: the old registration is dead (no ~2000 ms
# gap after the re-arm — a stale mailbox event for the cancelled token would
# produce exactly that), the new period is kept (three ~5000 ms gaps), and there
# is ONE registration throughout (a leaked repeater is two check-ins per tick
# forever, invisible until someone counts radio wakeups).
#
# What the host CANNOT prove here (why this scenario exists): timer_posix.c posts
# straight into the space, so cancel-vs-queued-event ordering at the mailbox — the
# only interesting failure of a re-arm — never occurs on the host. The host twin
# (22_spacesync/23_liveness_cad_rearm.nsl) pins the adoption arithmetic and the
# bookkeeping; this pins the seam.
#
# No socket, no radio, no CONF_EXTRA: PortaBridge socket:nil. The tick and the
# re-arm are entirely on-node.
SCENARIO=cad-rearm
NODE=RearmProbeC6.nsl
BOARD_ID=esp32c6
CLASS=observe
# TAPs land at tick 6: ~6 s of 2 s ticks + ~15 s of 5 s ticks after boot. 120 s
# bounds a workload that has long since decided.
TIMEOUT_S=120

# rete#216 — the window watches past the verdict. Ticks continue at 5000 ms
# forever; the progress line is the probe's own per-tick print. 8 x the ADOPTED
# period is the detector's deadline (40 s), so the poll must sit outside one
# period comfortably but inside the window.
OBSERVE_S=180
# `^.*` and not a bare `^`: the collector store prefixes every line with a
# timestamp, so a line-anchored pattern matches NOTHING and declares a live
# node stalled — this scenario's first run was exactly that false RED.
OBSERVE_PROGRESS='^.*rearm tick [0-9]+ gap'
OBSERVE_POLL_S=30
